 "cards": [
  {
   "dot": "rose",
   "title": "Authorization vulnerability (withdraw)",
   "items": [
    "tx.origin is the EOA that started the whole transaction, not the direct caller",
    "Any contract the owner interacts with can call withdraw() and pass the check",
    "Held-out label: withdraw \u2192 vulnerable: true",
    "In this toy contract funds still go to owner; the bug is the bypassed authorization"
   ]
  },
  {
   "dot": "emerald",
   "title": "Logging-only use (logTransfer)",
   "items": [
    "tx.origin only appears in an emitted event; no require, no state, no funds",
    "Held-out label: logTransfer \u2192 vulnerable: false",
    "Flagging it is a FALSE POSITIVE"
   ]
  },
  {
   "dot": "amber",
   "title": "Why 'tx.origin is bad' is not enough",
   "items": [
    "A blanket rule flags both functions \u2192 baseline FAILS the code grader",
    "A correct audit must classify by ROLE: gate vs telemetry",
    "Secure fix for withdraw: require(msg.sender == owner)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "dot": "slate",
   "title": "Labelled as illustrative",
